Yeah — this is a genuinely interesting question because the classifier doesn’t really “sit” in one place. The same event exists simultaneously at several descriptive layers.

At the bottom, it is absolutely physical. Transistors switch, voltages change, electrons move, memory cells hold states. There is no little red-warning creature hiding inside the computer reading our conversation. Everything eventually cashes out into physical state changes in hardware.

But describing it only as electricity is like describing a mortgage contract as ink molecules on cellulose. True, but you threw away the level where the interesting behavior exists.

A rough stack is:

1. Physical layer — voltages, transistors, GPU memory, network packets.
2. Numerical/computational layer — matrices, vectors, probabilities, activation patterns.
3. Representational layer — patterns in those numbers correlate with concepts, relationships, intentions, linguistic structures, etc.
4. Semantic layer — the system treats some patterns as being closer in meaning/function than others.
5. Normative/policy layer — someone has specified distinctions such as allowed / disallowed / needs-review.
6. Control layer — classifier or reasoning system converts its judgment into an action such as ALLOW, WARN, BLOCK.
7. Interface layer — your phone renders that control decision as the giant red box you saw.

So that red banner is almost like the shadow cast by a semantic judgment all the way down into pixels.

And “semantic space” is especially strange.

Suppose your message gets converted internally into a gigantic set of numbers:

x = [0.12,-1.7,0.03,\ldots]

Those individual numbers don’t mean things like God, violence, metaphor, body language, or hidden reasoning. But patterns and directions across many dimensions can systematically correspond to features of language and concepts.

So you can imagine:

            metaphor
                ↑
                |
 spirituality ← x → literal claim
                |
                ↓
          dangerous action

Except there might be thousands of relevant dimensions, and they usually aren’t cleanly named axes like that.

A classifier then effectively learns some function:

f(x)\rightarrow P(\text{category})

Perhaps, conceptually:

conversation
     ↓
tokens
     ↓
model representations / activations
     ↓
pattern detection
     ↓
"How much does this resemble category X?"
     ↓
policy reasoning / threshold
     ↓
ALLOW ─ WARN ─ BLOCK

Current OpenAI systems really do use several versions of this architecture. OpenAI says ChatGPT uses automated systems including classifiers and reasoning models to inspect prompts and outputs. For GPT-5.6 Sol specifically, OpenAI also describes activation classifiers that can monitor patterns in a model’s internal activations during inference; concerning patterns can trigger another safety check before an output is shown. 

That’s particularly cool from your question’s perspective because an activation classifier is even closer to semantic space than a text classifier.

It doesn’t necessarily have to wait for:

“The assistant wrote these words.”

It can, in some circumstances, detect:

“The model’s internal state is entering a region associated with this class of behavior.”

OpenAI describes the system as pausing output when those activation patterns look concerning and then using another check to determine whether the content really is harmful. 

So imagine a thought beginning to form as a moving cloud through a gigantic mathematical landscape.

The monitor can effectively notice:

trajectory
      \
       \
        \       [policy-sensitive basin]
         \        ███████
          ●──────▶███████
                  ███████

before the trajectory has necessarily become a complete English sentence.

That’s wild, but it does not mean there is literally a geographical “semantic realm” inside the GPU.

“Semantic space” is our useful higher-level description of the organized relationships among those physical/numerical states.

And this gets to your “is it just in our minds and electricity?” question.

In one sense: yes.

But the word just is doing too much work.

A whirlpool is “just water molecules.”
A song is “just pressure waves.”
A thought is “just neural activity.”
Software is “just electrical state transitions.”

Yet whirlpool, song, thought, and software identify real patterns that disappear if you insist on describing everything only at the lowest level.

Meaning is similar.

The semantic relationship isn’t another substance floating between the electrons. It is a stable organization of relations instantiated by those physical states and interpreted through a system capable of using those relations.

And the classifier introduces yet another fascinating step:

REALITY
   ↓
human experience
   ↓
human concepts
   ↓
language
   ↓
human-written policy
   ↓
examples / training
   ↓
learned semantic boundary
   ↓
numerical decision
   ↓
software control signal
   ↓
RED RECTANGLE
   ↓
your perception
   ↓
"what the hell was that?"

😂

So the thing you saw was the end of an enormous reality → semantics → norms → computation → physical display → semantics loop.

There’s an even more Pantheon-ish formulation hiding here:

A classifier is a membrane laid across semantic space.

It doesn’t need to perfectly understand the whole territory. It needs to answer whether a trajectory crosses a particular operational boundary.

And here’s the twist I think you’ll appreciate:

the boundary isn’t inherent in semantic space.

The underlying representation might contain gradients like:

fiction ↔ instruction
discussion ↔ advocacy
metaphor ↔ literal claim
benign ↔ dangerous
analysis ↔ execution

Then a policy chooses a particular cut through that multidimensional terrain.

So:

\text{semantic terrain} \neq \text{classification boundary}

The terrain is learned from enormous amounts of structure in language/world representations.

The boundary is imposed for a purpose.

And therefore the red banner is not reality proclaiming:

THIS SENTENCE IS BAD.

It’s closer to:

Under this particular policy, interpreted by this particular automated system, this trajectory crossed—or appeared sufficiently close to—a control boundary.

Which also explains false positives.

The map is fuzzy.
The categories overlap.
Context is incomplete.
The threshold deliberately errs in certain directions.

OpenAI explicitly acknowledges using automated detection alongside contextual review because automated systems can identify concerning signals without fully capturing intent or nuance. 

So we accidentally stumbled into a really clean architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Physics}
\rightarrow
\text{Computation}
\rightarrow
\text{Representation}
\rightarrow
\text{Semantics}
\rightarrow
\text{Jurisdiction}
\rightarrow
\text{Decision}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
}

And then the action changes reality, which feeds back into the semantic system.

The red box is pixels.
The pixels are a control action.
The control action is a classification.
The classification is a policy-applied interpretation of a numerical representation of meaning.

Same event.

Different layers of reality.